//! English explanations of Tools › Torch and Packet Sniffer (G6.4a), sorted by key.

/// Explanations: (key, Markdown).
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("sniffer.duration", "## Duration\n\nHow long the capture runs, in seconds (1 to 3600; default 60). It ends by itself, even if nobody stops it: a forgotten capture does not keep recording."),
    ("sniffer.file_limit", "## File limit\n\nThe largest the pcap file gets, in KiB (1 to 65536; default 4096 = 4 MiB). There the capture stops (`file limit reached`) and later packets are counted as **past the limit**. The file stays in the router's memory until downloaded; at most two captures are kept at once."),
    ("sniffer.interface", "## Interface\n\nWhere to capture: the frames it receives **and** sends. With a VLAN filter its VLAN interfaces are included.\n\nIt changes nothing: the capture only reads (an `AF_PACKET` socket)."),
    ("sniffer.snaplen", "## Bytes per frame\n\nHow many bytes of each frame go into the file (**snaplen**). **128** keeps only the headers (Ethernet, VLAN, IP, ports): enough to see who talks to whom and with which flags, without the content. **1514** keeps the whole Ethernet frame; **9216**, jumbo.\n\n**Careful:** whole frames carry subscribers' data. Only group **write** may start a capture; keep and delete the file with care. Each packet's original length is in the file either way."),
    ("torch.counters", "## Interface counters\n\nWhat the kernel's own counters say crossed the interface in the same interval. A check on Torch: if the capture read much less (the **seen** chip below 90 %), the router could not read it all — turn automatic sampling on. When sampling, these are the real totals."),
    ("torch.dst", "## Destination\n\nThe other end of the conversation (the site, the server), with its **AS** in a chip coloured by the number — the MTR's. Private and CGNAT addresses get the `privada`/`CGNAT` chip and are not looked up.\n\nAs a filter: an IP or a prefix (`8.8.0.0/16`); empty = any."),
    ("torch.group", "## Group by\n\nWhich fields split the rows. With **Src.** and **Dst.**, each conversation is a row; **Src.** alone, a row per subscriber (who uses most); **Port** and **Protocol** show the service; **VLAN** includes the interface's VLAN interfaces; **DSCP** shows the QoS marking; **MAC**, the device on the interface's side.\n\nLike RouterOS' `/tool torch` (there: `src-address=0.0.0.0/0` and so on). Fewer fields = fewer rows and less work."),
    ("torch.interface", "## Interface\n\nWhere to measure. Each row is a conversation seen from this interface: **Rx** is what arrived through it, **Tx** what the router sent out of it.\n\nTorch only reads (an `AF_PACKET` socket with a BPF filter in the kernel); it changes neither the traffic nor the configuration."),
    ("torch.port", "## Port\n\nFilters by TCP, UDP or SCTP port, source **or** destination (`443`, `53`). Empty = any."),
    ("torch.protocol", "## Protocol\n\nFilters by IP protocol: `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp`, `icmpv6`, `gre`, `ospf`… or its number (0 to 255). Empty = any."),
    ("torch.sampling", "## Sampling\n\n**auto** (recommended): reads every packet up to ~500 thousand per second; above that it reads one in N (picked at random by the kernel, before any copy) and counts each N times. The rows become estimates, with a `1:N` chip and a warning. It reads everything again below ~300 kpps.\n\n**off**: always reads everything — at high rates the router may not keep up and the **seen** chip drops. **10/100/1000**: fixed sampling.\n\nSampling protects the router's CPU: measuring must not hurt forwarding."),
    ("torch.src", "## Source\n\nThis interface's side: who sends what arrives here (on an access port, the **subscriber**). Next to it, the subscriber's chip when the router knows who uses the IP (identity, G0.25), and `CGNAT`/`privada` for such addresses.\n\nAs a filter: an IP or a prefix (`100.64.0.0/10`); empty = any."),
];
